Title: Let a TCP socket work without the completion-port bypass
Origin: electron-legacy (this repo)

On Windows XP every TCP socket failed before it was used:

  net: TCP over IPv4 loopback        FAIL
      listen EISDIR: illegal operation on a directory 127.0.0.1

for listen and connect alike, so no server, no HTTP and no fetch -- while UDP
and named pipes on the same machine were fine.

EISDIR is libuv's translation of ERROR_INVALID_FUNCTION, and that is winwrap's
answer to SetFileCompletionNotificationModes when it is asked for
FILE_SKIP_COMPLETION_PORT_ON_SUCCESS. The function is Vista's. The flag asks
that an operation which completes synchronously should not also queue a
completion packet, and XP has no such mode: it queues the packet regardless.
winwrap could say yes and does not, deliberately, because a caller told yes
stops waiting for the packet and may free the OVERLAPPED that XP then
completes into. Refusing costs an optimisation; agreeing costs a wild pointer.

uv__udp_set_socket() already takes that refusal for what it is and carries on
without UV_HANDLE_SYNC_BYPASS_IOCP. uv__tcp_set_socket() treats any failure of
the call as fatal to the socket. This gives it the same two lines. Without the
flag a TCP handle goes through the completion port for every operation, which
is the path libuv already takes for a socket with a non-IFS LSP installed, so
it is not a new mode being exercised for the first time.

No change on Vista and later, where the call succeeds.

For both builds: Electron's main process uses the same libuv sockets.

diff --git a/deps/uv/src/win/tcp.c b/deps/uv/src/win/tcp.c
--- a/deps/uv/src/win/tcp.c
+++ b/deps/uv/src/win/tcp.c
@@ -191,9 +191,18 @@
   if (!(handle->flags & UV_HANDLE_EMULATE_IOCP) && !non_ifs_lsp) {
     UCHAR sfcnm_flags =
         FILE_SKIP_SET_EVENT_ON_HANDLE | FILE_SKIP_COMPLETION_PORT_ON_SUCCESS;
-    if (!SetFileCompletionNotificationModes((HANDLE) socket, sfcnm_flags))
+    /* Not every Windows can do this. SetFileCompletionNotificationModes is
+     * Vista's, and where it is supplied for an older system the honest answer
+     * to FILE_SKIP_COMPLETION_PORT_ON_SUCCESS is ERROR_INVALID_FUNCTION: XP
+     * queues a completion packet for an operation that finished synchronously
+     * whatever it is told, so claiming otherwise would have the caller stop
+     * waiting for a packet that still arrives. The bypass is an optimisation
+     * and the socket works without it, through the completion port like any
+     * other; uv__udp_set_socket() has always treated this error that way. */
+    if (SetFileCompletionNotificationModes((HANDLE) socket, sfcnm_flags))
+      handle->flags |= UV_HANDLE_SYNC_BYPASS_IOCP;
+    else if (GetLastError() != ERROR_INVALID_FUNCTION)
       return GetLastError();
-    handle->flags |= UV_HANDLE_SYNC_BYPASS_IOCP;
   }
 
   if (handle->flags & UV_HANDLE_TCP_NODELAY) {
